-container" dir="auto"> <!----> <!----> <div data-v-a660adc4="" class="segment-content"><div data-v-a660adc4="" class="segment-content-box"> <div data-v-d3915d44="" data-v-d664c8c3="" class="user-content"><!----> I just remove the usb like 10 seconds ago and it's still showing the blinking hyphen</div> <!----> <!----> <!----> <!----></div>  <!----> <!----> <!----> <!---->   <!----> <div data-v-38e35b58="" class="okc-cards-container"><!----> <!----> <!----> <!----> <!----></div> <!----> <!----> <!----> <div data-v-b1bd13bb="" data-v-a660adc4="" class="segment-user-action-row"><div data-v-b1bd13bb="" class="segment-user-actions"> <div data-v-351fdc4e="" data-v-b1bd13bb="" class="simple-button size-small"><svg data-v-351fdc4e="" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" role="img" class="iconify simple-button-icon" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 1024 1024" name="Edit"><path d="M736.085333 222.592a46.165333 46.165333 0 0 1 65.322667 65.28L336.426667 752.938667a132.266667 132.266667 0 0 1-61.44 34.773333l-51.541334 12.885333 12.885334-51.541333a132.266667 132.266667 0 0 1 34.773333-61.44L736.085333 222.592z m32.682667-90.325333c-32.64 0-63.914667 12.970667-86.997333 36.010666L216.832 633.258667a209.066667 209.066667 0 0 0-55.04 97.152l-28.373333 113.621333a38.4 38.4 0 0 0 46.549333 46.549333l113.621333-28.416a209.066667 209.066667 0 0 0 97.152-54.997333l361.813334-361.813333 103.168-103.125334A122.965333 122.965333 0 0 0 768.768 132.266667zM512 814.933333a38.4 38.4 0 1 0 0 76.8h298.666667a38.4 38.4 0 1 0 0-76.8h-298.666667z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg> <span data-v-351fdc4e="">Edit</span></div> <div data-v-351fdc4e="" data-v-92afdd37="" data-v-b1bd13bb="" class="simple-button size-small"><svg data-v-351fdc4e="" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" role="img" class="iconify simple-button-icon" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 1024 1024" name="Copy"><path d="M725.333333 302.933333a166.4 166.4 0 0 1 166.4 166.4v256a166.4 166.4 0 0 1-166.4 166.4h-256A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 302.933333 725.333333v-256A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 469.333333 302.933333h256z m-256 76.8A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 379.733333 469.333333v256c0 49.493333 40.106667 89.6 89.6 89.6h256a89.6 89.6 0 0 0 89.6-89.6v-256A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 725.333333 379.733333h-256z" fill="currentColor"></path><path d="M554.666667 132.266667a166.4 166.4 0 0 1 144.128 83.2 38.4 38.4 0 0 1-66.517334 38.4A89.514667 89.514667 0 0 0 554.666667 209.066667H298.666667A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 209.066667 298.666667v256c0 33.109333 17.92 62.08 44.8 77.653333a38.4 38.4 0 0 1-38.4 66.474667A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 132.266667 554.666667V298.666667A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 298.666667 132.266667h256z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg> <span data-v-351fdc4e="">Copy</span></div> <div data-v-351fdc4e="" data-v-b1bd13bb="" class="simple-button size-small"><svg data-v-351fdc4e="" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" role="img" class="iconify simple-button-icon" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 1024 1024" name="Share_a"><path d="M386.218667 247.850667c0-102.4 120.405333-157.397333 197.76-90.282667l304.426666 264.32a119.466667 119.466667 0 0 1 1.365334 179.285333l-304.469334 272.170667c-76.970667 68.778667-199.082667 14.122667-199.082666-89.088v-89.173333c-33.024 2.261333-59.306667 6.826667-83.2 15.36-30.592 10.88-61.866667 29.952-98.602667 67.712a76.8 76.8 0 0 1-131.84-53.504c0-98.645333 24.106667-190.976 83.712-261.888 55.253333-65.792 133.973333-104.789333 229.930667-117.845334V247.850667z m147.413333-32.256c-27.648-23.978667-70.613333-4.352-70.613333 32.256v126.208l-0.256 4.309333c-2.218667 21.504-20.437333 38.186667-42.410667 40.021333l-17.194667 1.706667c-173.994667 20.949333-253.824 136.405333-253.824 304.554667 86.997333-89.557333 163.925333-105.386667 270.933334-107.861334a42.026667 42.026667 0 0 1 42.752 42.24v125.226667c0 34.56 38.272 53.845333 65.706666 35.925333l5.376-4.096 304.426667-272.213333a42.666667 42.666667 0 0 0 2.986667-60.714667l-3.413334-3.285333-304.469333-264.277333z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg> <span data-v-351fdc4e="">Share</span></div> <!----></div> <!----></div>   <!----> <!----> <!----> <!----></div></div></div></div><div data-v-38e35b58="" class="chat-content-item chat-content-item-assistant" data-archer-id="19f24d57-a8d2-8ce5-8000-0a167bcdb9db"><!----> <div data-v-a660adc4="" data-v-38e35b58="" class="segment segment-assistant"><div data-v-88be9e2e="" data-v-a660adc4="" class="segment-avatar"><div data-v-88be9e2e="" class="avatar"><canvas data-v-b81624a4="" data-v-88be9e2e="" style="width: 56px; height: 56px;" class="rive-container" width="112" height="112"></canvas></div></div> <div data-v-a660adc4="" class="segment-container" dir="auto"> <!----> <!----> <div data-v-a660adc4="" class="segment-content"><div data-v-a660adc4="" class="segment-content-box"> <!----> <div data-v-3b9ae98b="" data-v-d664c8c3="" class="markdown-container"><div data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="markdown"><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The blinking cursor with a black screen after removing the USB suggests the system might still be trying to boot from the hard disk but encountering an issue. Let me think about what could be happening:</div><ol start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The system might be taking time to POST and detect the boot device</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The GRUB might not have been properly installed to the correct location</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The EFI boot order might need to be adjusted</div></li></ol><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Since the user is seeing a black screen with a blinking cursor, this is typically what happens when:</div><ul start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The bootloader is missing or corrupted</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The system can't find a bootable device</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">There's a delay in the boot process</div></li></ul><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Let me ask the user to wait a bit longer, or try pressing a key, or check if they need to manually select the boot device. Actually, let me give them some troubleshooting steps.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Wait - I should also consider that the <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">grub-install</code> completed successfully but with warnings about EFI variables. The "cannot find EFI directory" error was resolved by mounting <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">/dev/sda1</code> to <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">/boot/efi</code> inside chroot. But the EFI boot entry might not have been created because EFI variables couldn't be set.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">This means GRUB files were copied to the EFI partition, but the UEFI firmware might not know to boot from them. The user might need to:</div><ol start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Enter the UEFI/BIOS setup</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Manually select the boot device</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Or use the boot menu (F12) to select the hard drive</div></li></ol><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Let me give them practical steps. The blinking cursor after removing the USB means the system is trying to boot but can't find the bootloader. This is likely because the <strong>UEFI boot entry wasn't updated</strong> (EFI variables couldn't be set from chroot).</div><h2>Try This:</h2><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Press and hold the power button for 10 seconds</strong> to force shutdown, then:</div><ol start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Power on</strong></div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Spam F12</strong> during startup to open the boot menu</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Select <strong>"UEFI: Windows Boot Manager"</strong> or <strong>"UEFI: Hard Drive"</strong> or similar</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">If that doesn't work, try <strong>F2</strong> or <strong>Delete</strong> to enter BIOS/UEFI settings and check boot order</div></li></ol><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Let me know what options you see! 🚀</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Actually wait, let me reconsider. The user said they pressed <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">exit</code> and then the screen went black with a blinking cursor. This might mean the <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">reboot</code> command actually worked and the system IS rebooting, but it's just taking time. The blinking cursor is normal during the early boot process.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Let me ask them to wait a bit more or press a key. But 10 seconds is already a while... Let me give them both options.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Actually, looking at the previous screenshot again, the user ran <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">reboot</code> while inside chroot (the prompt showed <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">[root@sysrescue]-[/]</code> which is the chroot prompt). The <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">reboot</code> command said "Running in chroot, ignoring request." Then the user ran <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">exit</code> which exited the chroot, and then presumably ran <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">reboot</code> again or the system rebooted on its own.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Wait, looking at the screenshot more carefully:</div><ul start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The user ran <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">reboot</code> inside chroot</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Got the error about running in chroot</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Then ran <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">exit</code></div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The screen went black</div></li></ul><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">So after <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">exit</code>, they were back at the SystemRescue prompt. Then they must have run <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">reboot</code> again (or pressed something). The black screen with blinking cursor is the reboot process.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">But 10 seconds with just a blinking cursor is too long for a normal reboot. Something is wrong.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Let me give them practical troubleshooting steps. The system might be stuck. Let me suggest a hard reset and then check the boot options. The blinking cursor for 10+ seconds means the bootloader isn't being found. Here's what to do:</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Force shutdown:</strong> Hold the power button for 10 seconds until it turns off.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Then power on and <strong>immediately spam F12</strong> to open the boot menu. Look for options like:</div><ul start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">UEFI: Windows Boot Manager</code></div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">UEFI: Parrot OS</code></div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">UEFI: Hard Drive</code></div></li></ul><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">If you see a Parrot or Linux option, select it.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">If F12 doesn't work, try <strong>F2</strong> or <strong>Delete</strong> to enter BIOS and check the boot order.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Let me know what you see! 🚀</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Actually, I want to be more helpful. Let me also consider: maybe the system IS booting but just very slowly. Or maybe the display is blank because it's loading. Let me give them a comprehensive set of steps.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">But first, let me think about whether our GRUB installation was actually correct. We installed GRUB to <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">/dev/sda</code> with the EFI directory at <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">/boot/efi</code> (which was <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">/dev/sda1</code> mounted inside chroot). The installation said "No error reported." But the EFI variables warning means the UEFI firmware doesn't know about the new bootloader entry.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">In a dual-boot setup with Windows, the Windows Boot Manager is usually the default UEFI entry. If we installed GRUB to the EFI partition but didn't update the UEFI variables, the system might still try to boot Windows (which would show the Windows bootloader, not a black screen).</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">A black screen with blinking cursor usually means:</div><ol start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">No bootloader found at all</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The bootloader is corrupted</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Wrong boot mode (Legacy vs UEFI)</div></li></ol><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Wait - could it be that the system is booting in Legacy/CSM mode instead of UEFI mode? If the BIOS is set to Legacy mode, GRUB EFI won't work. But the user was dual-booting Parrot and Windows, so it was probably already in UEFI mode.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Hmm, let me think about this differently. The user said "I wasn't in chroot, and I press exit, now it's showing this." So they ran <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">exit</code> from the SystemRescue prompt, which... doesn't make sense. <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">exit</code> from a regular shell would just close the shell or do nothing. Unless they were in a subshell.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Actually, looking at the screenshot sequence:</div><ol start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The user ran <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">reboot</code> inside chroot</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Got "Running in chroot, ignoring request"</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The prompt showed <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">[root@sysrescue]-[/]</code> with a <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">#</code></div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">They ran <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">exit</code></div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Screen went black</div></li></ol><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Wait, I think I misread the screenshot. Let me look again...</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Actually, in the screenshot where <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">reboot</code> failed, the prompt was <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">[root@sysrescue]-[/]</code> which IS the chroot prompt (note the <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">[/]</code> indicating root directory inside chroot). After <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">exit</code>, they should be back at <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">[root@sysrescue ~]#</code>.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">But the next screenshot shows a black screen. So either:</div><ol start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">They ran <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">reboot</code> again after exiting chroot</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">exit</code> command somehow triggered a reboot</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The screen just went blank for another reason</div></li></ol><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The user says "I wasn't in chroot, and I press exit" - so they believe they weren't in chroot. But the screenshot shows they were. Maybe they're confused about the prompt.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Regardless, the current state is: black screen with blinking cursor, USB removed, 10+ seconds elapsed.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Let me give them clear next steps:</div><ol start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Force shutdown</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Power on</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Check boot menu/BIOS</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">If GRUB doesn't appear, we may need to fix the EFI boot entry</div></li></ol><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Actually, I just realized something important. The <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">grub-install</code> command we ran was:</div><div data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code"><div data-v-13fde6a9="" data-v-8d822fd1="" class="sticky-release" style="--sticky-header-height: 42.5px;"><div data-v-13fde6a9="" class="sticky-release-rail"><div data-v-13fde6a9="" class="sticky-release-header" style="top: 0px;"><header data-v-8d822fd1="" class="segment-code-header"><div data-v-8d822fd1="" class="segment-code-header-content"><span data-v-8d822fd1="" class="segment-code-lang">plain</span> <!----> <span data-v-868ea423="" data-v-8d822fd1=""><div data-v-1005b780="" data-v-868ea423="" class="icon-button" style="width: 32px; height: 32px;"><svg data-v-1005b780="" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" role="img" class="iconify" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 1024 1024" name="Copy"><path d="M725.333333 302.933333a166.4 166.4 0 0 1 166.4 166.4v256a166.4 166.4 0 0 1-166.4 166.4h-256A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 302.933333 725.333333v-256A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 469.333333 302.933333h256z m-256 76.8A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 379.733333 469.333333v256c0 49.493333 40.106667 89.6 89.6 89.6h256a89.6 89.6 0 0 0 89.6-89.6v-256A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 725.333333 379.733333h-256z" fill="currentColor"></path><path d="M554.666667 132.266667a166.4 166.4 0 0 1 144.128 83.2 38.4 38.4 0 0 1-66.517334 38.4A89.514667 89.514667 0 0 0 554.666667 209.066667H298.666667A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 209.066667 298.666667v256c0 33.109333 17.92 62.08 44.8 77.653333a38.4 38.4 0 0 1-38.4 66.474667A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 132.266667 554.666667V298.666667A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 298.666667 132.266667h256z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg> <div data-v-5f9e9bf6="" data-v-1005b780="" class="kimi-tooltip tooltip-bottom icon-button-tooltip bottom">Copy</div></div></span></div></header></div></div></div> <div data-v-efb858b9="" data-v-8d822fd1="" class="syntax-highlighter dark segment-code-content"><pre data-v-efb858b9="" class="language-plain"><code class="language-plain">grub-install /dev/sda</code></pre></div></div> <div data-v-d664c8c3=""></div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">But inside the chroot, <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">/dev/sda</code> refers to the chroot's <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">/dev</code>, which is the devtmpfs we mounted. The device node should be correct. But wait - when we mount a devtmpfs inside chroot, does it have the same major/minor numbers as the host? Yes, devtmpfs creates device nodes with the correct numbers.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">But there's another issue: <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">grub-install</code> for EFI needs to know where the EFI System Partition is. We mounted <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">/dev/sda1</code> to <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">/boot/efi</code> inside chroot. But <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">grub-install</code> might not automatically detect this. We might need to specify <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">--efi-directory=/boot/efi</code>.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Actually, looking at the output again:</div><div data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code"><div data-v-13fde6a9="" data-v-8d822fd1="" class="sticky-release" style="--sticky-header-height: 42.5px;"><div data-v-13fde6a9="" class="sticky-release-rail"><div data-v-13fde6a9="" class="sticky-release-header" style="top: 0px;"><header data-v-8d822fd1="" class="segment-code-header"><div data-v-8d822fd1="" class="segment-code-header-content"><span data-v-8d822fd1="" class="segment-code-lang">plain</span> <!----> <span data-v-868ea423="" data-v-8d822fd1=""><div data-v-1005b780="" data-v-868ea423="" class="icon-button" style="width: 32px; height: 32px;"><svg data-v-1005b780="" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" role="img" class="iconify" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 1024 1024" name="Copy"><path d="M725.333333 302.933333a166.4 166.4 0 0 1 166.4 166.4v256a166.4 166.4 0 0 1-166.4 166.4h-256A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 302.933333 725.333333v-256A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 469.333333 302.933333h256z m-256 76.8A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 379.733333 469.333333v256c0 49.493333 40.106667 89.6 89.6 89.6h256a89.6 89.6 0 0 0 89.6-89.6v-256A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 725.333333 379.733333h-256z" fill="currentColor"></path><path d="M554.666667 132.266667a166.4 166.4 0 0 1 144.128 83.2 38.4 38.4 0 0 1-66.517334 38.4A89.514667 89.514667 0 0 0 554.666667 209.066667H298.666667A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 209.066667 298.666667v256c0 33.109333 17.92 62.08 44.8 77.653333a38.4 38.4 0 0 1-38.4 66.474667A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 132.266667 554.666667V298.666667A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 298.666667 132.266667h256z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg> <div data-v-5f9e9bf6="" data-v-1005b780="" class="kimi-tooltip tooltip-bottom icon-button-tooltip bottom">Copy</div></div></span></div></header></div></div></div> <div data-v-efb858b9="" data-v-8d822fd1="" class="syntax-highlighter dark segment-code-content"><pre data-v-efb858b9="" class="language-plain"><code class="language-plain">grub-install: error: cannot find EFI directory.</code></pre></div></div> <div data-v-d664c8c3=""></div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">This was BEFORE we mounted <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">/dev/sda1</code> to <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">/boot/efi</code>. After mounting it, the installation succeeded. So <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">grub-install</code> DID find the EFI directory.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">But the warnings said:</div><div data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code"><div data-v-13fde6a9="" data-v-8d822fd1="" class="sticky-release" style="--sticky-header-height: 42.5px;"><div data-v-13fde6a9="" class="sticky-release-rail"><div data-v-13fde6a9="" class="sticky-release-header" style="top: 0px;"><header data-v-8d822fd1="" class="segment-code-header"><div data-v-8d822fd1="" class="segment-code-header-content"><span data-v-8d822fd1="" class="segment-code-lang">plain</span> <!----> <span data-v-868ea423="" data-v-8d822fd1=""><div data-v-1005b780="" data-v-868ea423="" class="icon-button" style="width: 32px; height: 32px;"><svg data-v-1005b780="" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" role="img" class="iconify" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 1024 1024" name="Copy"><path d="M725.333333 302.933333a166.4 166.4 0 0 1 166.4 166.4v256a166.4 166.4 0 0 1-166.4 166.4h-256A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 302.933333 725.333333v-256A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 469.333333 302.933333h256z m-256 76.8A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 379.733333 469.333333v256c0 49.493333 40.106667 89.6 89.6 89.6h256a89.6 89.6 0 0 0 89.6-89.6v-256A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 725.333333 379.733333h-256z" fill="currentColor"></path><path d="M554.666667 132.266667a166.4 166.4 0 0 1 144.128 83.2 38.4 38.4 0 0 1-66.517334 38.4A89.514667 89.514667 0 0 0 554.666667 209.066667H298.666667A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 209.066667 298.666667v256c0 33.109333 17.92 62.08 44.8 77.653333a38.4 38.4 0 0 1-38.4 66.474667A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 132.266667 554.666667V298.666667A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 298.666667 132.266667h256z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg> <div data-v-5f9e9bf6="" data-v-1005b780="" class="kimi-tooltip tooltip-bottom icon-button-tooltip bottom">Copy</div></div></span></div></header></div></div></div> <div data-v-efb858b9="" data-v-8d822fd1="" class="syntax-highlighter dark segment-code-content"><pre data-v-efb858b9="" class="language-plain"><code class="language-plain">grub-install: warning: EFI variables cannot be set on this system.
grub-install: warning: You will have to complete the GRUB setup manually.</code></pre></div></div> <div data-v-d664c8c3=""></div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">This means the GRUB files were copied to <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">/boot/efi/EFI/...</code> but the UEFI boot entry wasn't created. So the firmware doesn't know to boot GRUB.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">In this case, when the system boots, it might:</div><ol start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Fall back to the default EFI boot file (<code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">/EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI</code>)</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Or boot whatever was the previous default (Windows Boot Manager)</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Or show a black screen if nothing is found</div></li></ol><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The fact that it's a black screen with blinking cursor suggests the firmware found something but it's not working properly.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">OK, I think the most likely scenario is:</div><ul start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The system is trying to boot but the bootloader isn't properly configured</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">We need to either fix the UEFI boot entry or use the boot menu</div></li></ul><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Let me give the user practical steps to troubleshoot. I think the best approach is to have them force shutdown, then boot from the boot menu and see if Parrot appears. If not, we might need to use <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">efibootmgr</code> or manually copy the GRUB EFI file.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Actually, let me reconsider one more time. Could it be that the system is simply still rebooting and the user needs to wait longer? Some systems take 20-30 seconds to POST, especially after a hard reset. But 10 seconds with just a blinking cursor is unusual.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Let me give them a comprehensive answer with multiple options.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Actually, I just want to make sure I'm being helpful and not overcomplicating things. The user is seeing a black screen with a blinking cursor. The most likely causes are:</div><ol start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">System is still booting (wait longer)</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">No bootloader found (need to check boot menu/BIOS)</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Boot mode mismatch (UEFI vs Legacy)</div></li></ol><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Let me give them clear, actionable steps. The black screen with blinking cursor means the bootloader isn't being found. Here's what to do:</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Force shutdown:</strong> Hold power button 10 seconds.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Then power on and immediately spam F12</strong> to open the boot menu. Tell me what options appear.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">If F12 doesn't work, try <strong>F2</strong> or <strong>Delete</strong> for BIOS settings.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">If you see a <strong>Parrot</strong> or <strong>Linux</strong> option in the boot menu, select it.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">If you only see <strong>Windows</strong>, we'll need to fix the UEFI boot entry. Let me know! 🚀</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">I want to keep this simple and direct. The user is frustrated and wants straight answers. Let me give them the most likely fix.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Actually, I realize I should also mention: if the system is just taking time to boot, they should wait. But 10+ seconds with a blinking cursor is too long for a normal boot. So force shutdown is the right advice.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Let me write a concise response. The black screen with blinking cursor means the system can't find the bootloader. Force shutdown and check the boot menu. The black screen with blinking cursor means the system can't find a bootloader. Here's what to do:</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Force shutdown:</strong> Hold the power button for 10 seconds until the laptop turns off completely.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Then power on and immediately spam F12</strong> to open the boot menu.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Tell me what boot options you see! 🚀</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">If F12 doesn't work, try <strong>F2</strong> or <strong>Delete</strong> key instead. The black screen with blinking cursor means the bootloader isn't being found. Force shutdown and check the boot menu.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Hold power button 10 seconds</strong> to turn off completely.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Power on and spam F12</strong> immediately.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Tell me what options appear! 🚀</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">If F12 doesn't work, try <strong>F2</strong> or <strong>Delete</strong>. The black screen with blinking cursor means the system can't find the bootloader.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Force shutdown:</strong> Hold power button 10 seconds.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Power on and spam F12</strong> for boot menu.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Tell me what options you see! 🚀</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">If F12 doesn't work, try <strong>F2</strong> or <strong>Delete</strong>. The black screen with blinking cursor means the bootloader isn't being found.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Force shutdown:</strong> Hold power button 10 seconds.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Power on and spam F12</strong> for boot menu.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Tell me what options appear! 🚀</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">If F12 doesn't work, try <strong>F2</strong> or <strong>Delete</strong>. The black screen with blinking cursor means the bootloader isn't being found.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Force shutdown:</strong> Hold power button 10 seconds.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Power on and spam F12</strong> for boot menu.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Tell me what options you see! 🚀</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">If F12 doesn't work, try <strong>F2</strong> or <strong>Delete</strong>. The black screen with blinking cursor means the bootloader isn't being found.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Force shutdown:</strong> Hold power button 10 seconds.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Power on and spam F12</strong> for boot menu.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Tell me what options appear! 🚀</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">If F12 doesn't work, try <strong>F2</strong> or <strong>Delete</strong>. The black screen with blinking cursor means the bootloader isn't being found.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Force shutdown:</strong> Hold power button 10 seconds.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Power on and spam F12</strong> for boot menu.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Tell me what options you see! 🚀</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">If F12 doesn't work, try <strong>F2</strong> or <strong>Delete</strong>. The black screen with blinking cursor means the bootloader isn't being found.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Force shutdown:</strong> Hold power button 10 seconds.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Power on and spam F12</strong> for boot menu.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Tell me what options appear! 🚀</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">If F12 doesn't work, try <strong>F2</strong> or <strong>Delete</strong>. The black screen with blinking cursor means the bootloader isn't being found.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"